{"step_index":3549,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-29T00:30:47Z","input_tokens":2418,"cache_read_tokens":50932,"output_tokens":5267,"thinking":"The code rendering requires modification to incorporate highlighting logic for the current execution step. Replacing the existing code lines with new code that implements step-specific highlighting is required. This ensures the correct code snippets are displayed with the appropriate visual cues during the execution process.\n\n\nThe function's objective is to add syntax highlighting to code lines. The line is first split into code and comment sections. Then, the code section is processed using a regular expression to identify and wrap tokens with appropriate HTML spans based on their type, such as strings, keywords, built-ins, and numbers. Finally, the highlighted code and comment sections are concatenated.\n\n\nThis new function determines which part of a code line should be highlighted during execution based on the current step and substep. First, it checks for sub-expressions within expression frames, prioritizing `highlightSub` and `expr` properties. If a condition is active, the condition's expression or left-hand expression is identified for highlighting. For assignment steps, the right-hand side or target of the operation is targeted for highlighting.\n\n\nAnalyzing different code statement types to identify the section requiring highlighting. Augmented assignments, like `c += nums[i]`, will highlight the expression after the operator. Return statements target the expression being returned.\n\nLoop statements will highlight either the iterable expression or the iteration variable. Function or method calls will highlight the entire call expression. General assignments highlight the right-hand side of the assignment.\n\n\nThe updated code includes a function to determine the code snippet to highlight, considering active steps and substeps. It checks different statement t